  "atomType": "T",
  "theory": {
        "intro": "Taking a logarithm undoes raising the same base to a power, so the two cancel: $\\log_{a}(a^{x})=x$ reads off the index, and $a^{\\log_{a}x}=x$ returns the number. The bases must match.",
        "facts": [
          "$\\log_{a}(a^{x})=x$ for all real $x$ — the logarithm returns the index.",
          "$a^{\\log_{a}x}=x$ for all $x>0$ — the base and its logarithm cancel.",
          "The cancellation only works when the base of the power and the base of the logarithm are the **same**.",
          "The index can be negative, a fraction or an algebraic expression: $\\log_{5}5^{-4}=-4$ and $\\log_{7}7^{2k}=2k$.",
          "A coefficient stays outside the identity, so $3\\log_{10}10^{4}=3\\times4=12$.",
          "To solve an equation, apply the identity so one side collapses to the unknown."
        ]
      },
  "practice": {
